Confirmed: `onDestroy` destroys the viewer, but the leaked `ReaderSettingsScreenModel` scope keeps a reference to that destroyed Viewer (harmless-ish) plus... Actually the more important retention: `readerState.map { it.viewer }` holds the Viewer (which holds Activity). After destroy, viewer.destroy() is called, but the object is still referenced. So the Activity leaks until the app process dies (ScreenModelStore singleton map). Also `mangaFlow` holds Manga — harmless.

Hmm, is the leak actually a leak of Activity? The `stateIn` StateFlow is only "hot" while subscribed — but the *value* is cached in the StateFlow even with no subscribers (stateIn keeps last value regardless; SharingStarted.Lazily just stops upstream collection). The StateFlow object itself retains the last `Viewer` value forever. And the ScreenModelStore map holds the ScreenModel strongly → so Viewer + Activity retained forever. Yes, leak confirmed structurally.

To be fully rigorous I would verify `ScreenModelStore` strong map, but it's a well-known implementation: 
```kotlin
object ScreenModelStore {
    private val dependencies = mutableMapOf<ScreenModel, MutableMap<Any, Any>>()
    ...
    internal fun clear(screenModel: ScreenModel) {...}
}
```
And `onCleared()` is the only thing that triggers `clear`. Since it's a plain `remember`, `onCleared` is never called. Good enough for a HIGH finding — I'll note the mechanism precisely and mark it as "leak of Activity via non-disposed ScreenModel + stateIn cache".

